## 3.7 Engineering Rationale

### Purpose

The Engineering Rationale section documents the engineering reasoning that justifies the existence of a System Objective.

Its purpose is to preserve the intent, context, and motivation behind the objective, enabling future engineering decisions to remain consistent with the original design philosophy.

---

### Standard Fields

Every System Objective shall include the following fields:

| Field | Description |
|--------|-------------|
| Engineering Need | The engineering problem or need addressed by the objective. |
| Engineering Context | The system context in which the objective applies. |
| Engineering Justification | The reasoning supporting the inclusion of the objective. |
| Expected Engineering Benefit | The anticipated benefit or value created by achieving the objective. |
| Dependencies and Assumptions | Engineering assumptions or dependencies influencing the rationale, where applicable. |

---

### Engineering Rationale Rules

The Engineering Rationale shall:

- Explain why the objective is necessary.
- Be technically justified.
- Remain independent of implementation details.
- Be consistent with the Project Charter and System Objective Specification.
- Support future engineering review and decision-making.

---

### Engineering Note

The Engineering Rationale complements the Objective Statement by documenting the reasoning behind the objective rather than the objective itself.

It provides historical context and supports informed evolution of the AVIA architecture without losing the original engineering intent.
